> No, the primary purpose of gain energy, as the power analogue of
> healing/extra healing/full healing, is to restore power. 

Not according to the vanilla code, it's not.  From potion.c:

	num = rnd(5) + 5 * otmp->blessed + 1;
	u.uenmax += (otmp->cursed) ? -num : num;
	u.uen += (otmp->cursed) ? -num : num;
			
In short, "num" is set to somewhere between 2-6 for (un)cursed potions,
and 7-11 for blessed potions.  Then the user's maximum energy is
increased by that amount, as is the user's current energy.  (Or
decreased, if the potion was cursed.)

The effect of an extra 11 points of current Pw is "one magic missile".

The effect of an extra 11 points of _maximum_ energy will be reflected
throughout the game, and is far more significant; for lower-level
characters, this will make an even larger difference.

> increasing of maximum power points is the same as healing's increasing
> of HP, which goes back to Rogue.  Gaining maximum power, I say, should
> be considered a side-effect.

Again, not according to the code; right now the only useful effect from
quaffing a potion of gain energy is the gain for your maximum power.

For a wizard with 300+ Pw, getting an extra 11 when you're low is
completely worthless.  If the Dev Team intended the potion to restore
_current_ power as its primary purpose (which I seriously doubt), they 
screwed up bigtime.

